/-!
# Radius images at critical distance four

In the literal full point-core branch, the preceding distance-four reduction
makes every critical seed image cyclic of order two, generated by a
transposition. All seeds in a radius-two ball lie in one incident edge core.
That marked elementary point core has a unique transposition, so their
images generate the same cyclic subgroup. Odd-color generators are already
contained in the neighboring even-color seeds. Thus every two-edge radius
image has order at most two.

On a critical path of length four, critical noncontainment and the proved
radius escape give the two lower bounds. Both midpoint radius images
therefore have order exactly two. The midpoint radius and the opposite
critical seed have the same image under the original symmetric quotient;
their commutators with the initial seed coincide. This identifies the actual
noncentral critical line without discarding the order-32 cover's center.

The selected-span application supplies the index premise of the existing
terminal natural-quotient construction, retaining its nonabelianness,
derived-fixedness and endpoint-action premises. The chosen-replacement
application proves distance two whenever a two-edge radius image is larger
than two, and explicitly retains the remaining small-radius alternative.
The full distance-four contradiction and the other radical-image branches
remain obligations of the large-distance assembly owner.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, *Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two*
(1993), (5.8) and the full point-core specialization of (5.12)(2)--(3),
printed pp.919--920 and 925--927. The two-edge radius argument uses the
existing marked point-core transposition theorem and the original amalgam
action, without identifying a cover with its natural quotient.
-/

public noncomputable section
